Rewrite the first test to check dash code points on every line, and hyphen patterns only in prose (comments and strings). I'll extract prose:

```ts
function proseChunks(text: string): string[] {
  const chunks: string[] = []
  const withoutBlocks = text.replace(/\/\*[\s\S]*?\*\//g, (chunk) => { chunks.push(chunk); return ' ' })
  const withoutComments = withoutBlocks.replace(/\/\/[^\n]*/g, (chunk) => { chunks.push(chunk); return ' ' })
  const stringPattern = /'(?:[^'\\\n]|\\.)*'|"(?:[^"\\\n]|\\.)*"|`(?:[^`\\]|\\.)*`/g
  let match: RegExpExecArray | null
  while ((match = stringPattern.exec(withoutComments)) !== null) {
    chunks.push(match[0].replace(/\$\{[^}]*\}/g, ''))
  }
  return chunks
}
```

Then hyphen patterns checked on chunks. Note: my own test source will contain these regexes with "'-" ... e.g. `/'(?:[^'\\\n]...` — does the test source contain ' - '? In the stringPattern regex: `[^'\\\n]` etc. No ' - '. But `'-'.repeat(2)` fine. Also the regex literal contains `//` which... the block/line comment stripper would treat `\/\*` fine but a line containing `//` inside a regex would be stripped as a line comment — that's OK since we only push chunks; it may swallow code after `//` on that line in the *source under test* (judgeMode.ts), but that's just a heuristic. However it could also strip part of my own test file's regex line. Since I only check chunks for hyphen patterns, over-stripping reduces sensitivity slightly; acceptable.

Careful: line comment regex `\/\/[^\n]*` would match inside strings like 'https://...' — judgeMode has none.

Let's rewrite the test.

